    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p9_b8",
      "text": "Insights from these training dynamics reveal that while a\nlarger \u03b1 in LBR accelerates early bias suppression, main-\ntaining such a high penalty may lead to over-constraint as\nthe model converges. To balance this, we further investigate\na dynamic scheduling strategy for the regularization weight\n\u03b1. Specifically, we employ a Cosine Annealing schedule,\ninitializing \u03b1 at 1 \u00d7 10\u22124 and decaying it to 1 \u00d7 10\u22126.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.44,
        394.66,
        291.1,
        476.43
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        221,
        1578,
        1164,
        1905
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": true,
      "char_count": 407,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p9_b9",
      "text": "As shown in Tables 8 and 9, this dynamic approach (LBR\n(Cosine)) achieves superior performance across most bench-\nmarks compared to the fixed-weight baseline. These results\nunderscore the strong potential of our method and highlight\nadaptive regularization as a promising direction for future\nresearch. However, to maintain the narrative flow, we do not\nelaborate further on other dynamic scheduling algorithms\nin this work.\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.08,
        484.25,
        291.09,
        578.05
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        1936,
        1164,
        2312
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.04,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 424,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
