      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.96,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Medi",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 13,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p9_b12",
      "text": "In this work, we systematically analyze language bias in\nLVLMs, tracing the model\u2019s over-reliance on its language\nmodality to a core misalignment in training dynamics, where\nprocesses like VIT and DPO often prioritize textual im-\nprovements over visual alignment. Based on this finding,\nwe propose two targeted interventions: Language Bias Reg-\n",
      "bbox": [
        55.08,
        645.66,
        291.09,
        715.63
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        220,
        2582,
        1164,
        2862
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 10.06,
      "font_name": "NimbusRomNo9L-Regu",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 344,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.25036_p9_b13",
      "text": "ularization (LBR) for VIT and Language Bias Penalty\n(LBP) for DPO. Experiments demonstrate that these simple\ntraining modifications consistently improve general capabil-\nities (LBR) and significantly reduce hallucinations (LBP)\nwithout introducing any additional data or auxiliary models.\nUltimately, this work offers both a deeper understanding of\nlanguage bias and a practical path toward more reliable and\naligned LVLMs.\n",
      "bbox": [
        307.08,
        364.68,
        543.19,
        458.5
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
